Subject: BET: Announcing a New Bet--Resolution!

(This stuff has only a little to do with straight Extropianism, but it
loosely relates to betting schemes, logarithms, etc. At least it's not
about flying Yogis or the natural rights of (uploaded) Martians!)

A couple of people have commented in one way or another on the "bet" I
described, so I'll use the most recent one, by Steve Whitrow to respond
further. 

As you may have gathered, the odds are not what they seem at first. This is
something I discovered in 1969-70, when I was in high school, in a column
or article in "Scientific American." I took the "Collect on 1, 2, 3, or 4"
side of the bet with some friends of mine and actually won a few bucks. (I
was sneakier this time, taking the other side of the bet, as I knew the bet
would not get acted on.)

Eli Brandt was the only person to say he'd take the bet (except he put some
kind of qualification on it, something about taking the populations of
cities with strictly fewer than 100K people). Alexander Chislenko also
commented (and made an interesting comment about the number of subparts of
anything being close to Pi. I suspect it's actually related to e, but this
is off the subject for now.).

>Let's say the numbers are populations of cities taken at random from a 
>gazetteer. In this case I believe Tim would lose by having to pay out on
>numbers starting with 1, 2, 3 or 4.
>
>I make the true odds as: 
>                             1, 2, 3 or 4 ............. 1.512 / 1 on
>                             5, 6, 7, 8 or 9 .......... 1.512 / 1 against

I don't follow this. The odds are not even. There are actually _more_
numbers (from the sources I listed in the original post) beginning with 1,
2, 3, or 4 than those beginning with the remaining five digits. This is
counterintuitive, but is true.

>By obtaining a price of evens for the 1/2/3/4 hits I reckon the opponent
>would have secured a return of around 20.4% for himself, on total turnover.
>And he is laying the 5/6/7/8/9 at only evens when it should be around 6 / 4.
>
>However, in the case of number of pages in books, size of postings, etc, I
>think Tim would have the odds in his favour, an 11.1% return.

No, actually, I would've lost rather badly.

How can there be more numbers beginning with the 4 lower digits than the 5
higher digits?

First, for truly random numbers (like "0.741035" and "0.18486943"), all
digits are equally represented. This is why I excluded random numbers.

Consider a thought experiment. This will help to clarify the phenomenon.

Alice and Bob play the following game: 

Alice collects a penny for each number beginning with a 1, 2, 3, or 4. Bob
collects on each number beginning with a 5, 6, 7, 8, or 9.

They start counting up from 1.

>From 1 to 49, Alice is ahead of Bob. By as much as 49 cents. And about 25
cents "on average" (meaning, if the game got stopped at some random point).

>From 50 to 99, Alice is still ahead for "most" of the time, and Bob only
pulls ahead by 10 cents when the number 99 is reached. If the game got
interrupted at any random time during "Bob's numbers," he'd be behind on
average.

>From 100 to 499, Alice pulls way ahead!

>From 500 to 999, Bob only catches up at around 900.

Here's a table:

Regime              Alice       Bob       Alice - Bob

1-4                    4              0          4
5-9                    4              5           -1
10-49                44            5          39
50-99                44           55         -11
100-499            444         55         389
500-999            444         555       -111
1000-4999        4444       555       3889
5000-9999        4444       5555     -1111

...and so on. Alice is ahead at nearly point in the game, while Bob is
struggling to catch up.

This phenomenon shows up in some strange ways:

* there are "more" cities with populations beginning with 1, 2, 3, or 4
than that begin with the remaining 5 digits. Likewise for many other
distributions.

* this was "discovered" (though Gauss probably knew about it :-} ) when an
engineer was walking down a hallway at Oak Ridge with a book of logarithms
in his hand (for those born after 1960, there used to be entire books of
logs). He glanced down at the pages and noticed that the pages at the
beginning, covering the smaller numbers, were "dirtier" than the later
pages. Even accounting for how people flip open books, this suggested that
for some odd reason engineers were more frequently looking up the logs of
numbers beginning with low digits.

* the analysis confirmed this. Machined parts or widgets had dimensions,
weights, whatever, distributed in the way I've described here, so in fact
the engineers were consulting the log tables on an unequal basis.

* in fact, the distribution is exactly logarithmic. Look at how much length
the digits occupy on a slide rule (5-9 are compressed to the right) and
you'll see exactly the ratios.

Hope you like this. Next time, I'll describe how the number e relates to
which motel you ought to stop at, or which marriage proposal to accept.

-Tim


