>Said colonel had informed the city officials that it is against the law
>(I would suppose Federal law) to use the military equipment in a civilian
>situation such as that.

>Anyone know the status of that law? 

>Phil

The courts have traditionally viewed National Guard troops as state
employees, under the command of state governors, unless they are called
into federal service.  It is not uncommon for governors to use National
Guard troops in conditions of civil unrest.  See, for example, the facts
underlying *Engblom v Carey,* 677 F2d 957 (2nd Cir 1982) (National Guard
troops activated by Governor of NY in response to strike of state's prison
guards).

I would suppose that Bradley carriers are quite common in the National
Guard.

I know of no law prohibiting the President from deploying National Guard
troops or DOD forces in response to civil unrest.  I recall that Bush was
responsible for sending troops to LA during the riots, and to Florida after
the recent hurricane.  This is arguably a natural extension of the
president's power as commander-in-chief of the armed forces.

Regardless of its legality, I deplore the use of the National Guard or
other armed forces in civil situations.  They are not trained to respect
citizens' rights, and even the police who do have such training fail to
heed it all to often.


