behavior as you describe would happen spontaneously.

(I won't get into the touchy subject of public displays of nudity or sexual
behavior, as this pushes a lot of buttons (so to speak). Just remember that
many people find various behaviors abominable and grotesque--but our
society does not ban those behaviors. Examples (for me at least):
fundamentalist religious preachers, Hair Club for Men, television ads for
douches, etc. But if I see something I don't like, I flip the channel. If I
still see nothing I like, I read a book.)

Now what was your point about free speech? Do you think all tasteless or
boorish behavior should be banned, outlawed? In this country, much bizarre
behavior is not banned (at least not yet). People cope by picking their
friends carefully and self-selecting the events they attend. Private
propery allows people to set the rules on their property.

And free speech does not mean someone else has to subsidize your
statements, give you your own radio show, or allow you to "perform" at
weddings. What it really means is that various levels of government are not
to make laws about what is and what is not acceptable speech, writing, or
thought. (There are some obvious exceptions, like obscenity, espionage,
libel, etc. Some of us are very worried that more and more things are being
added to this category.)

>   Can anybody explain me the sacred value of speech / info and answer the
>above questions?

In my (admittently unusual) opinion, the core values of America have a kind
of "Calvinist" flavor to them, in the sense that Americans tend to let each
person go to hell in the way they choose. If my neighbor wants to smoke,
and I know it'll kill him eventually, I say "Fine, it's your choice."
Sometime this is phrased as "I am not my brother's keeper" (for a good
anti-Soviet education corrective, read Ayn Rand's "Atlas Shrugged." She may
not have had all the answers, but she cured a lot of people of the idea
that they have to sacrifice themselves for the collective good.). I say
"Calvinist" because in some sense it's "wrong" to make choices for other
people...they must learn to make the right choices themselves, and if they
don't...well, evolution in action.

Sometimes this view is phrased as "To each their own." (translation: let
each person choose his own path in life). Or, "None of your damned
business." The phrase I like the most is "Live and let live." 

Well, I hope this helps. Absorb the real American foundational ideas--not
the crap you see on t.v. today--and you'll see why the First Amendment is
so primal, so basic to everything else. 

-Tim May





